 35SI  L 3611      8                                                           ?
 35SI cL $2014St18 observed a surprising |g transition at 3611 keV, which must  
 35SI2cL depopulate a state that is unbound to neutron emission by at least 1.1 
 35SI3cL MeV, considering S(n)=2470 {I40} (2021Wa16).                           
 35SI cL $One possible explanation is this {+35}Si level is populated by 1n     
 35SI2cL knockout from the deeply-bound 1d{-5/2} orbit in {+36}Si and followed  
 35SI3cL by the emission of the other 1d{-5/2} neutron, resulting in a 2p-2h    
 35SI4cL configuration dominated by two holes in the deeply bound neutron       
 35SI5cL 1d{-5/2} orbit. Such a configuration should have a small overlap with  
 35SI6cL the ground state in {+34}Si that has a large contribution of 0p-0h     
 35SI7cL configurations, leading to a significantly hindered neutron decay. The 
 35SI8cL |g decay, on the other hand, would be a fast 5/2+ |) 7/2- g.s. E1 decay
 35SI9cL and could potentially compete with the neutron emission.               
 35SI cL $A second possible explanation could be that this state is weakly      
 35SI2cL populated in a more complicated multistep reaction pathway. The        
 35SI3cL 3611-keV |g results predominantly from events with residue momenta in  
 35SI4cL the tail of the momentum distribution. The low-momentum tail is        
 35SI5cL associated with more inelastic reactions, which would be consistent    
 35SI6cL with a multistep process.                                              
 35SI cL $Partial knockout |s=0.8 mb {I2}.                                      
 35SI  G 3611      8  1.0    2                                                 ?
                                                                                
